So, if we denote the radius of the large circle as \(R\), the distance from the center of the large circle to the center of a small circle is \(R - 3\). However, this distance can also be described as half the distance across the regular hexagon formed by the centers of the six small circles, which is simply the side length of the hexagon (the distance between the centers of two adjacent small circles) because the radius of the large circle is equivalent to extending from the center through a small circle's radius to its edge.

Thus, the radius of the large circle \(R\) is the sum of the distance from the center of the large circle to the center of a small circle and the radius of the small circle:
